WEBVTT

00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:01.069
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Part 1

00:00:02.254 --> 00:00:05.675
<v><b>R:</b></v> …her story is one of determination and hard graft,

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:11.217
which took her from leaving school at 15 with nothing to the icon of elegant living she is today.

00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:13.263
But, Jo, what went wrong with your schooling?

00:00:13.586 --> 00:00:16.988
<v><b>J:</b></v> Um, I was dyslexic – I am dyslexic, horribly – and it, but it wasn’t…

00:00:17.212 --> 00:00:19.701
it wasn’t discovered until I’d left school.

00:00:19.726 --> 00:00:24.069
So, I can remember at the age of 11 cheating in an exam because

00:00:24.109 --> 00:00:28.333
I was so fed up being the bottom in the class, and so I looked at a multiple choice,

00:00:28.358 --> 00:00:31.807
and I could copy the multiple choice. And I was caught, rightly so,

00:00:32.176 --> 00:00:34.836
and made to stand on my chair, and the teacher turned round and said to me,

00:00:34.866 --> 00:00:37.708
‘You are lazy and stupid, and you’ll never make anything of your life.’

00:00:38.205 --> 00:00:42.139
I don’t blame her, you know, what she was – she was dealing with a situation at the time

00:00:42.365 --> 00:00:45.656
– but I knew I was neither. I was smart, I was clever, I was hard-working,

00:00:45.943 --> 00:00:48.267
I just learned in a very different fashion.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:49.536
<v><b>R:</b></v> Because that can go either way, can’t it?

00:00:49.548 --> 00:00:52.430
You can either, sort of, take that on board and feel crushed by it, or you can think,

00:00:52.446 --> 00:00:53.704
‘Actually, I’m not accepting that.’

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:57.396
<v><b>J:</b></v> I think I’ve lived my life not being defined by other people’s opinions of me.

00:00:57.669 --> 00:00:59.448
Everyone’s always got an opinion of you, haven’t they?

00:00:59.464 --> 00:01:02.934
They…and you can either allow yourself to be defined by that, or you move on.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.592
And I knew – I knew I was always two steps ahead, so, I could make face creams,

00:01:08.608 --> 00:01:12.821
I could do all sorts of different things, so, I could do things that other children couldn’t do.

00:01:12.895 --> 00:01:14.669
<v><b>R:</b></v> Great knowledge to have for an entrepreneur, isn’t it?

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:15.909
And you did start out very early; I mean,

00:01:15.921 --> 00:01:18.252
you were on market stalls selling your dad’s paintings and stuff,

00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:21.488
and learning the rudiments of, um, how to encourage people to buy.

00:01:21.513 --> 00:01:23.135
<v><b>J:</b></v> Yeah, I loved that, absolutely.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.234
So, I had three jobs when I was little – one was the market stall.

00:01:26.588 --> 00:01:28.227
So, I would go from the age of seven.

00:01:28.362 --> 00:01:32.059
I’d go with my dad to Crayford, Dartford, Blackheath, Tunbridge Wells,

00:01:32.454 --> 00:01:37.336
and he was a painter, and we came from a very poor background,

00:01:37.361 --> 00:01:38.539
so we had lived in a two-up, two-down,

00:01:38.564 --> 00:01:43.449
and as I would walk through the door on a Saturday morning to go to the market my mum would say,

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:47.257
‘There’s no food in the fridge, Jo. If you don’t sell a painting, there is nothing to eat.’

00:01:47.523 --> 00:01:48.596
So, I knew my job…

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:49.311
<v><b>R:</b></v> No pressure!

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:51.338
<v><b>J:</b></v> Well…But, you know something?

00:01:51.357 --> 00:01:54.172
When you grow up like that, it feels like the most natural thing for you.

00:01:54.197 --> 00:01:58.198
You don’t feel like, you know, ‘poor’. I didn’t ever feel ‘poor me’, by the way, ever.

00:01:58.638 --> 00:02:01.602
So, I knew I would always have to sell the first painting very quickly, and I did,

00:02:01.627 --> 00:02:05.393
and I’d pocket the money, knowing that we had enough to pay the rent,

00:02:05.418 --> 00:02:07.915
or, you know, certainly put a couple of meals in the fridge.